-- Q365: Quality Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Governance and leadership of the firm","Engagement performance and documentation","Acceptance and continuance of client relationships","Monitoring and remediation of deficiencies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SQMS No. 1.30, the acceptance and continuance component requires the firm to evaluate management integrity, competence, resources, and ethical compliance before accepting new clients or continuing existing relationships. This component directly addresses the process described in the stem. Wrong (A): Governance and leadership addresses firm culture and tone at the top, not client evaluation decisions. Wrong (B): Engagement performance governs how individual engagements are conducted, not the client acceptance decision. Wrong (D): Monitoring and remediation involves inspecting completed work and correcting deficiencies, not evaluating prospective clients.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 365;

-- Q1129: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["40 hours total over two years","80 hours total, with 24 in government auditing","120 hours total over two years","60 hours total, with 20 in government topics"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GAGAS Chapter 4, auditors must complete 80 hours of CPE every two years, with at least 24 hours directly related to the government environment or government auditing and at least 20 hours in each year of the two-year period. Wrong (A): 40 hours is the annual minimum under many state boards but does not meet the GAGAS two-year CPE requirement. Wrong (C): 120 hours exceeds the GAGAS standard and is not required by Government Auditing Standards. Wrong (D): 60 hours with 20 in government topics does not match the GAGAS requirement of 80 total with 24 in government auditing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1129;

-- Q1130: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A report on the entity''s overall operating efficiency","A report on internal control and on compliance with laws","A management letter with recommendations for improvements","A report comparing entity performance to industry benchmarks"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): GAGAS requires auditors to report on internal control over financial reporting, including significant deficiencies and material weaknesses, and on compliance with laws, regulations, contracts, and grant agreements. These reports go beyond the standard GAAS opinion. Wrong (A): GAGAS does not require a report on operating efficiency; the additional reports focus on internal control and compliance. Wrong (C): A management letter is optional and advisory, not a required GAGAS report. Wrong (D): GAGAS does not require performance benchmarking reports in a financial audit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1130;

-- Q1131: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All federal programs must be tested regardless of their size","Only programs with expenditures exceeding $1,000,000 are tested","Programs are classified as Type A or B, then risk-assessed for selection","The auditee selects which federal programs the auditor will test"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.518), programs are classified as Type A or Type B based on expenditure thresholds. The auditor then applies risk assessment to determine which programs are tested as major programs, targeting at least 40% of total federal expenditures (20% for low-risk auditees). Wrong (A): Not all federal programs are tested; the risk-based approach selects only major programs for compliance testing. Wrong (B): The $1,000,000 figure is not the defined threshold; Type A classification uses the larger of $750,000 or a percentage of total awards. Wrong (D): The auditor, not the entity, determines which programs are classified as major programs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1131;

-- Q1320: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the CPA begins fieldwork at the client''s office","When the CPA signs the initial engagement letter only","At the earlier of beginning attest services or signing the letter","When the financial statements under audit are publicly issued"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.200.010, the period of the professional engagement begins when the CPA either signs the engagement letter or begins performing attest services, whichever is earlier. Independence must be maintained from that point through the report date. Wrong (A): The period starts before fieldwork commences, at engagement acceptance or the start of attest services. Wrong (B): Signing the letter alone is insufficient; the period also begins if attest services start before signing. Wrong (D): Report issuance marks the end of the independence period, not its beginning.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1320;

-- Q1325: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Obtain pre-approval from the AICPA Ethics Division for conflicts","Apply a checklist of prohibited activities and relationships only","Identify threats, evaluate significance, and apply appropriate safeguards","Automatically decline any engagement where a threat is identified"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.000.010, the conceptual framework requires CPAs to identify threats to independence, evaluate each threat''s significance, and apply safeguards to reduce threats to an acceptable level. If safeguards are insufficient, the CPA must decline the engagement. Wrong (A): The framework does not require pre-approval from a regulatory body; it relies on the CPA''s professional judgment. Wrong (B): The conceptual framework is principles-based, not a rigid checklist of prohibitions. Wrong (D): Identifying a threat does not automatically require declining the engagement; safeguards may adequately reduce the threat.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1325;

-- Q1330: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Self-review threat from evaluating own work","Advocacy threat from promoting client positions","Undue influence threat from external pressure","Self-interest threat from fee dependence on client"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.210.010, a self-interest threat arises when a firm derives significant revenue from a single client. The firm may be reluctant to challenge management to avoid losing the lucrative relationship, impairing objectivity. Wrong (A): A self-review threat arises when a CPA evaluates their own prior work, not from revenue concentration. Wrong (B): An advocacy threat arises when a firm promotes or supports a client''s position, not from fee dependence. Wrong (C): An undue influence threat involves external pressure on the auditor, which is distinct from the firm''s own financial self-interest.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1330;

-- Q1353: Professional Responsibilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Advertises CPA services through various social media platforms","Offers professional services at fees lower than competitors charge","Provides a second opinion to a prospective client upon request","Retains client records as leverage to collect unpaid fees"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.400.200, retaining client records to enforce fee collection is an act discreditable to the profession. CPAs must not withhold records that clients need to meet legal or regulatory obligations, even when fees remain unpaid. Wrong (A): Advertising CPA services on social media is permissible under the AICPA Code and is not discreditable. Wrong (B): Competitive pricing is allowed; the Code does not prohibit offering services at lower fees. Wrong (C): Providing a second opinion to a prospective client is a permissible professional activity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1353;

-- Q1364: Professional Responsibilities [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disregard the predecessor''s workpapers and begin the audit fresh","Report the predecessor auditor to the state board immediately","Withdraw from the engagement due to the client''s fraud history","Evaluate the evidence, extend procedures, and communicate with governance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 510.09, the successor auditor must evaluate evidence from the predecessor''s workpapers when they reveal significant matters such as fraud. AU-C 240.41 requires assessing whether the fraud has current-period implications and communicating findings to those charged with governance under AU-C 260. Wrong (A): Ignoring the predecessor''s evidence would violate the auditor''s professional obligations to evaluate all relevant information. Wrong (B): Reporting to the state board may be premature before the auditor understands the scope and current relevance of the fraud. Wrong (C): Automatic withdrawal is not required; the auditor should first evaluate the impact on the current engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1364;

-- Q1365: Professional Responsibilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Maximize shareholder returns for all of their audit clients","Prioritize client interests above all other considerations","Serve the public trust including clients, creditors, and government","Ensure that audit fees remain affordable for all companies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 0.300.030, the public interest principle recognizes that CPAs have obligations to multiple stakeholders, including clients, creditors, investors, the business community, and government. The public trust is paramount and distinguishes the profession. Wrong (A): Maximizing shareholder returns is a corporate objective, not the CPA''s public interest obligation. Wrong (B): Prioritizing only client interests conflicts with the CPA''s broader duty to serve the public trust. Wrong (D): The public interest principle concerns serving multiple stakeholders, not regulating the affordability of audit fees.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1365;

-- Q1368: Professional Responsibilities [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confidentiality of client information","Due care in performing services","Acts discreditable to the profession","Scope and nature of services offered"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.400.001, a felony conviction involving financial fraud constitutes an act discreditable to the profession. Such conduct undermines public confidence in the accounting profession and may require reporting to the state board. Wrong (A): Confidentiality governs protection of client information, not criminal conduct by staff members. Wrong (B): Due care relates to competence and diligence in performing professional services, not criminal behavior. Wrong (D): Scope and nature of services addresses the range of services a CPA may provide, not staff misconduct.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1368;

-- Q1374: Professional Responsibilities [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept full responsibility for the specialist''s findings without evaluation","Require the specialist to carry their own professional liability insurance","Disclaim responsibility for audit portions that relied on the specialist","Evaluate the specialist''s qualifications, work performed, and results"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 620, when using the work of a specialist, the auditor must evaluate the specialist''s professional qualifications, understand the nature and scope of the work, and evaluate whether the findings are appropriate for audit objectives. The auditor integrates the specialist''s work into overall audit evidence. Wrong (A): The auditor cannot blindly accept the specialist''s findings; evaluation of qualifications and results is required. Wrong (B): Requiring personal liability insurance is not an auditing standard requirement for using a specialist. Wrong (C): The auditor does not disclaim responsibility; the auditor remains responsible for the audit opinion even when using a specialist.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1374;

-- Q1375: Professional Responsibilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confidentiality of client information","Public interest and stakeholder duty","Acts discreditable to the profession","Competence and due care in practice"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code ET 0.300.060, CPAs must maintain competence in areas where they practice, including keeping current with industry developments, standards, and regulatory changes. Failing to update industry knowledge for three years directly violates the competence component. Wrong (A): Confidentiality concerns protecting client information, not maintaining professional knowledge. Wrong (B): Public interest is a broader principle about serving stakeholders, not specifically about keeping skills current. Wrong (C): Acts discreditable involves conduct that harms the profession''s reputation, not failure to maintain technical competence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1375;

-- Q1376: Professional Responsibilities [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Raise the concern and, if unresolved, follow firm conflict resolution","Comply with the partner because the partner holds ultimate authority","Immediately report the partner directly to the AICPA for review","Comply with the instruction but document the disagreement in workpapers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 220 and the AICPA Code ET 0.300.060, audit team members must exercise professional judgment and not subordinate it improperly. The staff accountant should raise the concern to the partner and, if unresolved, follow the firm''s internal conflict resolution procedures or consult senior personnel. Wrong (B): The partner''s authority does not override professional standards; complying with an instruction that compromises audit quality is improper subordination. Wrong (C): Reporting directly to the AICPA bypasses the firm''s internal procedures and is premature before attempting resolution internally. Wrong (D): Documenting the disagreement while complying still results in insufficient audit evidence, which is not acceptable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1376;

-- Q1378: Quality Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Maximize firm profitability through efficient resource allocation","Guarantee that all audit opinions issued by the firm are correct","Provide reasonable assurance that responsibilities and standards are met","Ensure compliance with all client requests regarding audit procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): SQMS No. 1 establishes that the objective of a quality management system is to provide the firm with reasonable assurance that the firm and its personnel fulfill their responsibilities and that engagements are performed in accordance with professional standards. The standard provides reasonable, not absolute, assurance. Wrong (A): Maximizing profitability is a business objective, not the purpose of a quality management system. Wrong (B): The standard provides reasonable assurance, not a guarantee that all opinions are correct. Wrong (D): The system ensures compliance with professional standards, not with individual client requests.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1378;

-- Q1384: Quality Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The engagement quality reviewer for each engagement","The firm''s CEO or managing partner or equivalent","The AICPA Peer Review Board as external overseer","Each individual engagement partner for their clients"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SQMS No. 1, ultimate responsibility for the system of quality management is assigned to the firm''s CEO, managing partner, or equivalent. While individual partners have engagement-level responsibilities, overall system design and operation is a leadership responsibility. Wrong (A): The engagement quality reviewer evaluates specific engagements but does not bear ultimate responsibility for the entire quality management system. Wrong (C): The AICPA Peer Review Board conducts external reviews but does not bear responsibility for a firm''s internal quality management system. Wrong (D): Individual engagement partners are responsible for their engagements, not the firm-wide quality management system.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1384;

-- Q1387: Quality Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Establish policies for evaluating client acceptance and continuance","Accept all potential clients to maximize the firm''s revenue","Accept only clients that are referred by existing firm clients","Continue all client relationships unless the client requests termination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SQMS No. 1, the acceptance and continuance component requires the firm to establish policies for evaluating prospective and existing clients. Key considerations include management integrity, the firm''s competence and resources, ethical compliance including independence, and whether the engagement can be performed per professional standards. Wrong (B): Accepting all clients without evaluation would violate this component by ignoring quality considerations. Wrong (C): Limiting acceptance to referrals alone is not a quality management standard; evaluation criteria under SQMS No. 1 are broader. Wrong (D): Continuing all relationships without evaluation contradicts the requirement to reassess existing client relationships.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1387;

-- Q1388: Quality Management [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disregard the finding since documentation is secondary to quality","Investigate root causes and implement remedial actions to correct it","Immediately terminate the engagement partners who were responsible","Report the documentation finding directly to the SEC for review"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): SQMS No. 1 requires the firm to investigate root causes of identified deficiencies and implement appropriate remedial actions. For documentation gaps, this may include additional training, revised templates, and enhanced supervisory review to address the underlying cause. Wrong (A): Ignoring documentation deficiencies violates SQMS No. 1, which requires the firm to address and remediate identified deficiencies. Wrong (C): Immediate termination is disproportionate; remediation should be constructive and address the root cause first. Wrong (D): Documentation deficiencies identified in internal inspections are resolved internally, not reported to the SEC.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1388;

-- Q1391: Quality Management [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the team''s judgment since the reviewer should not override","Override the engagement team''s conclusion and revise the report","Complete the review despite concerns to avoid delaying the report","Discuss the concern with the partner; withhold concurrence if unresolved"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SQMS No. 2, the engagement quality reviewer must discuss significant concerns with the engagement partner. If those concerns remain unresolved, the reviewer must not provide concurrence, and the report cannot be issued until the matter is resolved. Wrong (A): Passively accepting the team''s judgment would undermine the purpose of the engagement quality review process. Wrong (B): The reviewer does not have authority to override the engagement team''s conclusions or alter the audit report. Wrong (C): Completing the review while ignoring significant concerns would violate the reviewer''s responsibility under SQMS No. 2.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1391;

-- Q1394: Quality Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Focus exclusively on profitability targets and growth objectives","Delegate all quality management responsibilities to junior staff","Foster a quality culture through actions, messages, and resources","Establish governance structures identical to those of public companies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SQMS No. 1, the governance and leadership component requires firm leadership to promote an internal culture that recognizes quality as essential. Leadership must demonstrate commitment through actions, allocate sufficient resources, and establish accountability for quality at all levels. Wrong (A): Focusing exclusively on profitability directly contradicts the requirement to balance quality considerations with commercial objectives. Wrong (B): Delegating all quality responsibilities to junior staff fails to demonstrate the leadership commitment SQMS No. 1 requires. Wrong (D): SQMS No. 1 does not require replicating public company governance structures; it requires leadership commitment appropriate to the firm.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1394;

-- Q1395: Quality Management [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The report can be released with the reviewer''s objection in a memo","The reviewer must resign from the review to avoid causing delays","The team must resolve the concern before the reviewer approves release","The matter should be deferred to the next annual inspection cycle"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SQMS No. 2, the engagement report cannot be dated or released until the engagement quality review is completed, including resolution of all matters raised by the reviewer. The team must perform additional procedures or provide support to satisfy the reviewer''s concern. Wrong (A): Releasing the report with an unresolved objection violates SQMS No. 2, which requires all concerns to be resolved before issuance. Wrong (B): The reviewer cannot resign to avoid delay; the review must be completed with all concerns addressed. Wrong (D): Going concern uncertainties require timely resolution and cannot be deferred to a future inspection.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1395;

-- Q1399: Quality Management [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue a firm-wide policy reminder without addressing the partner","Remove the partner from the firm immediately without further steps","Reduce the partner''s compensation without providing an explanation","Provide targeted coaching, enhanced review, and follow-up monitoring"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SQMS No. 1, effective remediation addresses both systemic issues and individual performance gaps. Targeted coaching, enhanced supervisory review of the partner''s engagements, and follow-up monitoring are proportionate and constructive responses to documentation deficiencies. Wrong (A): A firm-wide reminder alone does not address the individual partner''s specific and repeated documentation failures. Wrong (B): Immediate removal is disproportionate unless the deficiency reflects incompetence or unwillingness to improve after remediation efforts. Wrong (C): Reducing compensation without explanation does not address the root cause and fails to provide constructive remediation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1399;

-- Q1410: Quality Management [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Risks are identified, then one or more responses address each risk","Each quality risk must have exactly one corresponding response only","Responses are designed first, then risks are identified to justify them","Quality risks and responses operate independently of each other"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SQMS No. 1, the firm identifies and assesses quality risks and then designs and implements one or more responses to address each assessed risk. Multiple responses may address a single risk, and a single response may address multiple risks. Wrong (B): There is no one-to-one requirement; a single risk may require multiple responses, and one response can address several risks. Wrong (C): The process flows from risk identification to response design, not the reverse; designing responses before identifying risks would be fundamentally flawed. Wrong (D): Quality risks and responses are directly linked; responses must be designed specifically to address identified risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1410;

-- Q1425: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the entity''s management team as the primary recipient","Only the Government Accountability Office for federal oversight","The general public through a formal press release announcement","Those charged with governance and, where required, oversight bodies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GAGAS paragraphs 6.40-6.41, auditors must report findings of fraud, noncompliance, and significant deficiencies to those charged with governance and to officials of the organizations requiring or arranging the audit. Written communication ensures oversight bodies can take corrective action. Wrong (A): Communicating only to management without including those charged with governance would not satisfy GAGAS reporting requirements. Wrong (B): Findings are not reported solely to the GAO; the primary recipients are those charged with governance and the arranging officials. Wrong (C): GAGAS does not require public press releases; findings are communicated through formal audit reports to designated parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1425;

-- Q1427: Government Auditing Standards [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$250,000 regardless of total federal awards expended","$500,000 as a flat threshold for all auditees","The larger of $750,000 or 3% of total awards, with a ceiling","10% of total federal awards expended by the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.518), a Type A program has expenditures exceeding the larger of $750,000 or a percentage of total federal awards (3% for entities with up to $100 million in total awards), subject to a ceiling. This tiered threshold determines major program classification. Wrong (A): $250,000 does not correspond to any Uniform Guidance threshold for Type A program classification. Wrong (B): $500,000 is not the defined threshold; the base amount is $750,000 combined with the percentage-based calculation. Wrong (D): 10% of total federal awards is not the Type A threshold; the actual percentages are much smaller and tiered by total expenditure level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1427;

-- Q1431: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["30 days after receipt or 9 months after period end, whichever is earlier","60 calendar days after the end of the entity''s fiscal year","90 calendar days after the audit has been fully completed","120 calendar days after the end of the entity''s fiscal year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): The Uniform Guidance requires the reporting package, including the SF-SAC, to be submitted to the Federal Audit Clearinghouse within 30 calendar days after receipt of the auditor''s report or within 9 months after the audit period ends, whichever is earlier. Wrong (B): 60 days after fiscal year-end is not the correct deadline; the trigger is receipt of the auditor''s report, not the fiscal year-end date. Wrong (C): 90 days after audit completion does not match the Uniform Guidance requirement, which specifies 30 days after receipt of the report. Wrong (D): 120 days after fiscal year-end is not a recognized deadline under the Uniform Guidance submission requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1431;

-- Q1434: Government Auditing Standards [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Total federal expenditures must be below $1 million annually","The entity must be a state government to qualify for status","Unmodified opinions with no material weaknesses for two prior years","The entity must have fewer than 100 employees to qualify"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.520), low-risk auditee status requires unmodified opinions on financial statements and compliance for major programs in the two most recent audit periods, with no material weaknesses. Low-risk auditees benefit from reduced coverage at 20% of total federal expenditures. Wrong (A): There is no expenditure ceiling for low-risk status; the criteria focus on audit opinion quality and internal controls, not spending levels. Wrong (B): Low-risk status is not limited to state governments; any non-federal entity meeting the criteria may qualify. Wrong (D): Employee count is not a criterion for low-risk auditee status under the Uniform Guidance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1434;

-- Q1435: Government Auditing Standards [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An efficiency finding regarding resource utilization","An internal control deficiency finding only","A financial statement misstatement finding only","A compliance finding for noncompliance with grant terms"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Using federal funds for unauthorized purposes is a compliance finding under GAGAS, specifically noncompliance with the terms and conditions of the grant agreement. The questioned costs must be quantified and reported in the schedule of findings and questioned costs. Wrong (A): Efficiency findings relate to resource utilization, not unauthorized use of grant funds for impermissible purposes. Wrong (B): While an internal control deficiency may also exist, the primary finding is noncompliance with grant provisions, not solely an internal control issue. Wrong (C): This is fundamentally a compliance issue regarding grant terms, not merely a financial statement misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1435;

-- Q1436: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only financial reporting requirements for each major program","All 12 compliance types for every program regardless of applicability","Only procurement and suspension or debarment requirements","Types with direct and material effect per the Compliance Supplement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Uniform Guidance, the auditor tests compliance requirements identified in the OMB Compliance Supplement that could have a direct and material effect on each major program. Not all 12 types apply to every program; the auditor determines applicability based on program-specific requirements. Wrong (A): Financial reporting is only one of up to 12 types of compliance requirements; limiting testing to this alone would be insufficient. Wrong (B): Testing all 12 types regardless of applicability is unnecessary and not required; the auditor identifies which types are relevant to each program. Wrong (C): Procurement and suspension/debarment are only two of the potential compliance requirement types and may not be the only applicable ones.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1436;

-- Q1439: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the fee charged for the nonaudit services provided","Threats identified, safeguards applied, and independence rationale","A blanket statement that no independence threats exist","Nothing, because documentation is not required for nonaudit work"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): The Yellow Book requires auditors to document their evaluation of threats to independence when performing nonaudit services. This must include specific threats identified, safeguards applied, and the rationale supporting the conclusion that independence is not impaired. Wrong (A): Documenting only the fee is insufficient; GAGAS requires documentation of the full threat assessment and safeguard analysis. Wrong (C): A blanket statement without specific analysis does not satisfy the Yellow Book''s documentation requirements for nonaudit services. Wrong (D): Documentation is expressly required under GAGAS when performing nonaudit services; failing to document would be noncompliant.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1439;
